 497 (1957)"],"authors":[{"full_name":"Misner, C.W."}]},"curated_relation":false,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/44851"},"legacy_curated":true},{"reference":{"label":"2","publication_info":{"journal_volume":"2","artid":"604","year":1957,"page_start":"604","journal_title":"Annals Phys."},"authors":[{"full_name":"Wheeler, J.A."}]},"curated_relation":false,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/42729"}},{"reference":{"label":"3","misc":["Trans. Am. Math. Sot. 27, 106 (1925)"],"authors":[{"full_name":"Rainich, G.Y."}]}},{"reference":{"imprint":{"publisher":"Springer"},"label":"4","publication_info":{"journal_volume":"8","artid":"14","page_start":"14","journal_title":"Nature"},"title":{"title":"Raum Zeit Materie"},"misc":["New York, 1950. 6. J. L. SYNGE No. 1 in the University of Toronto Studies, Applied Mathematics Series. Univ. of Toronto Press, Toronto, 1935. 6. J. L. SYNGE p. 326 Publishing Company, Amsterdam, 1956. 7. W. B. BONNOR Proc. Phys. Sot. A67, 225 (1954). 8. LOUIS MARIOT, thesis Chapter IV. University of Paris, mimeograph, privately reproduced, April 6, 1957. 24 See Power and Wheeler (67) for a table of the analogies between geometrodynamics and hydrodynamics, and the problems that are suggested by this analogy. 23 In this connection see papers by Everett, Misner, and Wheeler (68). CLASSICAL GEOMETRODYNAMICS 601 9. A. EINSTEIN 3rd ed., p. 107. Princeton Univ. Press, Princeton, 1950. 10. BERNHARD RIEMANN (1826-1866), Habilitationsvorlesung of June 10, 1854 on entry into the philosophical faculty of the University of Gottingen in B. Riemann H. Weber, ed 2nd ed. Reprinted by Publications, Inc., New York, 1953. 11. CLIFFORD (1873). 12. H. WEYL 4th ed., Section 34 Berlin, 1921"],"authors":[{"full_name":"Rainich, G.Y."}]}},{"reference":{"label":"4","publication_info":{"journal_volume":"48","artid":"73","year":1935,"page_start":"73","journal_title":"Phys.Rev."},"title":{"title":"Philosophy of Mathematics and Natural Science"},"misc":["quotation from Weyl’s (translated by Olaf Helmer), p. 91. Princeton Univ. Press, Princeton, New Jersey, 1949. 13"],"authors":[{"full_name":"Einstein, A."},{"full_name":"Rosen, N."}]},"curated_relation":false,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/44701"}},{"reference":{"publication_info":{"journal_volume":"29","artid":"641","page_start":"641","journal_title":"Gen.Rel.Grav."}},"curated_relation":false,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/42396"},"legacy_curated":true}],"number_of_pages":79,"referenced_authors_bais":["C.W.Misner.1","Nathan.Rosen.1","G.Lemaitre.1","Albert.Einstein.1","J.A.Wheeler.1"],"legacy_version":"20160801160953.0","legacy_creation_date":"2002-05-22","preprint_date":"1957","author_count":2,"first_author":{"full_name":"Misner, Charles W.","last_name":"Misner","first_name":"Charles W.","recid":997278},"control_number":42728,"dois":[{"value":"10.1016/0003-4916(57)90049-0"}],"earliest_date":"1957","document_type":["article"],"texkeys":["Misner:1957mt"],"abstracts":[{"source":"Elsevier","value":"If classical physics be regarded as comprising gravitation, source free electromagnetism, unquantized charge, and unquantized mass of concentrations of electromagnetic field energy (geons), then classical physics can be described in terms of curved empty space, and nothing more. No changes are made in existing theory. The electromagnetic field is given by the Maxwell square root of the contracted curvature tensor of Ricci and Einstein. Maxwell's equations then reduce, as shown thirty years ago by Rainich, to a simple statement connecting the Ricci curvature and its rate of change. In contrast to unified field theories, one then secures from the standard theory of Maxwell and Einstein an already unified field theory. This purely geometrical description of electromagnetism is traced out in detail. Charge receives a natural interpretation in terms of source-free electromagnetic fields that (1) are everywhere subject to Maxwell's equations for free space but (2) are trapped in the worm holes of a space with a multiply-connected topology. Electromagnetism in such a space receives a detailed description in terms of the existing beautiful and highly developed mathematics of topology and harmonic vector fields. Elementary particles and real masses are completely excluded from discussion as belonging to the world of quantum physics.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"Elsevier"}}],"refereed":true,"titles":[{"title":"Classical physics as geometry: Gravitation, electromagnetism, unquantized charge, and mass as properties of curved empty space"}],"external_system_identifiers":[{"schema":"SPIRES","value":"SPIRES-4933389"},{"schema":"MSNET","value":"93387"}],"facet_author_name":["983692_John Archibald Wheeler","997278_Charles W. Misner"],"_oai":{"sets":["Literature"],"id":"oai:inspirehep.net:42728","updated":"2023-10-03T08:44:09.364287"},"curated":true,"journal_title_variants":["Annals Phys."]},"created":"2002-05-22T00:00:00+00:00"}],"total":2},"links":{"self":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=t%20%22Gravitation%22%20and%20a%20Misner&size=15&page=1","bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=t%20%22Gravitation%22%20and%20a%20Misner&size=15&page=1&format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=t%20%22Gravitation%22%20and%20a%20Misner&size=15&page=1&format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=t%20%22Gravitation%22%20and%20a%20Misner&size=15&page=1&format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=t%20%22Gravitation%22%20and%20a%20Misner&size=15&page=1&format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=t%20%22Gravitation%22%20and%20a%20Misner&size=15&page=1&format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=t%20%22Gravitation%22%20and%20a%20Misner&size=15&page=1&format=cv"}}